Mica follows one constraint: Git is the source of truth. Losing the Mica server must never mean losing website content.

## The path

The model is a plain TypeScript module. Astro imports it to create typed collections. An Astro build also writes the model as data to `.mica/schema.json`.

Mica reads that model from the repository and generates its navigation, tables, and forms. A save lands in a draft workspace: your checkout, or the Git clone of the self-hosted editor. All editors see the drafts. In the local editor it is your checkout: the Save button writes files, and Publish makes a Git commit with your own `git`. In the self-hosted editor it is a Git clone on the data volume, and a draft is a file in that clone. Publish commits the changes that the editor chooses as one commit and pushes it over HTTPS. The push starts the existing site build.

The website never calls Mica. Mica never needs to know where the website is hosted. They meet in the repository.

## The packages

- `@micacms/core` defines fields, validation, serialization, and the files on disk.
- `@micacms/astro` connects the model and content to the Astro Content Layer.
- `@micacms/runtime` is the API server, with no authentication of its own. It also contains the file access for the local editor and the Git clone.
- `@micacms/editor-ui` is the editing interface, shipped as built assets.
- `@micacms/auth-oidc` is the OpenID Connect gate for deployments.

A website installs `@micacms/core` and `@micacms/astro`, and `@micacms/editor` as a development tool. These are the public packages. The other packages are internal: the two applications install them.

Two applications compose them. `@micacms/editor` is the local one: loopback only, no authentication, no user, no database, and it refuses an address on another machine. `@micacms/self-hosted` is the Docker image, behind OpenID Connect, with its own Git clone on the data volume. It fetches and fast-forwards on its own, and never merges. It pushes to any Git host over HTTPS. Git history shows the editor as author and Mica as committer.

See [Choose how to run Mica](/docs/choose-how-to-run/) for how to pick one.

## Atomic changes

An entry can include JSON, rich text, and many media files. A publish writes the chosen changes in one commit or writes nothing. A stale revision gives a conflict response and leaves the editor's changes in the form.

## Deliberate limits

Mica has no content API, GraphQL layer, media server, build webhook, page builder, or password system. These limits keep the public website independent and the editing system small.

## Next

To run the self-hosted editor, go to [Self-hosting](/docs/self-hosting/).
